"Implicit Visual Compression with Diffusion Foundation Models (https://compressionasadaptation.github.io/)\nciteturn32203view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://compressionasadaptation.github.io/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 60\nL0: # Compression as Adaptation: Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Zongyu Guo†zongyuguo.github.io ^{1*}, cite1†Jiajun He†jiajunhe98.github.io ^{2*}, cite2†Zhaoyang Jia†scholar.google.com ^{1}, cite3†Xiaoyi Zhang†scholar.google.com ^{1}, Jiahao Li^{1}, Xiao Li^{1}, Bin Li^{1} cite4†José Miguel Hernández-Lobato†jmhl.org ^{2} Yan Lu^{1}\nL3: \nL4: ^{1}Microsoft Research Asia, ^{2}University of Cambridge ^{*}Equal contribution\nL5: \nL6: cite5†Paper†arxiv.org cite6†arXiv†arxiv.org cite7†Code†github.com L7: ## Compression Performance Comparison\nL8: \nL9: Drag to compare Ground Truth (left) and the selected compressed result (right).\nL10: \nL11: [Button: Beauty] [Button: RaceHorse] [Button: BasketballDrive]\nL12: \nL13: ## Beyond Compression: Editing\nL14: \nL15: cite8†Image: Editing results using LoRA-based representations. We can use the LoRA-based representations for controlled generation, such as image editing or merging\nL16: \nL17: Video editing results by changing the dog in the prompt to panda\nL18: ## Method overview\nL19: \nL20: cite9†Image: Comparison between different representation methods. L21: \nL22: Comparison between different representation methods: (a) Explicit representations by encoding signals into symbolic latent variables. (b) Implicit representations that encode signal information implicitly in functions. (c) Adaptation of generative models can serve as implicit visual representations\nL23: \nL24: cite10†Image: A detailed illustration of the adaptation method in a pretrained diffusion foundation model. L25: A detailed illustration for our methods by adaptation in a pretrained diffusion foundation model\nL26: ## Representation results\nL27: \nL28: cite11†Image: Reconstruction quality versus training step results. Reconstruction quality v.s. training step. (a) Common LoRA representations with different ranks for image Kodim03 from Kodak dataset. (b) One-vector representations for image Kodim03, varying LoRA rank and vector size after hashing. (c) One-vector representations for video Beauty from UVG dataset.\nL29: ## Compression Performances\nL30: \nL31: cite12†Image: UVG compression performance results. Comparisons video codecs on UVG. For DISTS, FVD and LPIPS, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better.\nL32: ## Compression inference-time scaling\nL33: \nL34: We identity a key advantage of functional representation: it supports inference-time scaling for better performance naturally. In our framework, we can generate multiple samples per denoising steps, and select the most promising one for better compression quality.\nL35: \nL36: cite13†Image: Compression inference-time scaling results. Reconstruction quality by inference-time scaling with different number of steps and different sample sizes per step.\nL37: ## Composing Representations of Multiple Videos\nL38: \nL39: Concatenation of LoRA for different videos enables composition of multiple videos.\nL40: \nL41: ShakeNDry's background + Beauty's object and motion.\nL42: \nL43: ShakeNDry's object + Beauty's background and motion.\nL44: \nL45: Merging ShakeNDry and Beauty in the background of Beauty.\nL46: \nL47: Merging ShakeNDry and Beauty in the background of ShakeNDry.\nL48: ## More RD Curves\nL49: \nL50: cite14†Image: HEVC B compression performance results. Comparisons video codecs on HEVC B. For DISTS, FVD and LPIPS, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better. cite15†Image: HEVC C compression performance results. Comparisons video codecs on HEVC C. For DISTS, FVD and LPIPS, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better. cite16†Image: HEVC E compression performance results. Comparisons video codecs on HEVC E. For DISTS, FVD and LPIPS, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better.\nL51: ## BibTex\nL52: \nL53:     `@inproceedings{he2026compression,\nL54:       title={Compression as Adaptation: Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models},\nL55:       author={Zongyu Guo and Jiajun He and Zhaoyang Jia and Xiaoyi Zhang and Jiahao Li and Xiao Li and Bin Li and José Miguel Hernández-Lobato and Yan Lu},\nL56:       year={2026},\nL57:       booktitle={International Conference on Machine Learning},\nL58:     }`\nL59: This website is borrowed from the cite17†Nerfies†nerfies.github.io website and licensed under a cite18†Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License†creativecommons.org .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn32203view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/CompressionAsAdaptation/CompressionAsAdaptation.github.io/commits?per_page=100\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://api.github.com/repos/CompressionAsAdaptation/CompressionAsAdaptation.github.io/commits?per_page=100 is not accessible via this tool.\n"
